{
  "abstract": "Yolcu, Aaronson and Heule reduced Collatz residue-avoidance statements to relative termination of string rewriting systems and left the classes $\\{1\\},\\{5\\},\\{7\\},\\{5,7\\}$ mod 8 open. We prove that two i.i.d. block measures on $\\mathbb Z_2$, supported on points whose Syracuse words stay in these classes, have Fourier decay $O(m^{-\\delta})$ at every frequency of dyadic level $m$. The proof transposes Tao's pairing argument: a constant commutator between two inverse branches, a cosine product over swapped pairs, and a local limit theorem for a 3-adic unit. By Davenport-Erdos-LeVeque, each open class therefore contains a 2-adic point with normal binary digits and positive liminf drift, along whose orbit every block frequency exists. Next we show that, for each open class, no arctic (max-plus) interpretation that reads numbers from the least significant end and satisfies an end-readability condition (C-end) proves relative termination, in one stage or with rule removal. The proof compares the interpretation on two integers built from a normal witness. Below the leading block, both integers look random to the automaton, but the image is longer by about $k\\log_23-S$ bits and must still receive a smaller value. Removing (C-end) is reduced to an end-completion problem for automata reading the progression $3^ky+e$, which we state with the known obstructions. For certificates of zero-entropy type we prove two exact lemmas. The resolution needed to exclude one rational cycle by residue-graph certificates is $D(C)+2$. Arctic interpretations whose binary part consists of labelled simple cycles cannot prove any open class. Integer survival counts up to $2^{32}$, and in residue trees up to $2^{72}$, match the 2-adic Markov model within a factor $1.3$ in all four classes. No open class is settled. The results identify normal points of positive drift as the obstruction that a certificate must separate from the integers.",
